WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:04.470 size:75% position:38% line:0%
Today, we're at CalTech,

00:00:04.503 --> 00:00:05.772 align:start position:0% line:0%
because in a building
over there

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:08.307 align:start position:0% line:0%
is a camera that absolutely
blows my mind.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:10.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, we've filmed
at some very high frame rates.

00:00:10.309 --> 00:00:12.378 align:start size:66% position:34%
We're talking up to
about half a million,
which is...

00:00:12.411 --> 00:00:14.280
- Not to be sniffed at.
- ...serious frame rates.

00:00:14.313 --> 00:00:17.083 align:start size:56% position:44%
Their camera
puts ours to shame

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:19.485 align:start size:63% position:38%
and does 10 trillion
frames per second.

00:00:19.518 --> 00:00:21.354 align:start position:0%
That's 13 zeroes.

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:24.757 align:start position:0%
For reference,
that is 20 million times faster

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:27.326 align:start position:0%
than the fastest we've
ever filmed on this channel.

00:00:27.359 --> 00:00:28.494 align:start size:63% position:38%
And there's not much
you can't film

00:00:28.527 --> 00:00:30.129 align:start size:63% position:38%
with half a million
frames a second,

00:00:30.162 --> 00:00:32.432 align:start size:72% position:28%
but one of those things
is the speed of light.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:33.666 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Not a bad subject.
- No.

00:00:33.699 --> 00:00:35.368 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Let's get in there.
- All right.

00:00:47.546 --> 00:00:50.483 align:start position:0%
Even the shoe cover
technology is cool.

00:00:50.516 --> 00:00:52.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
I kind of want one of these
at home, actually.

00:00:52.551 --> 00:00:54.587 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Just for people.
- All right, let's go in,

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.222
see what this CUP
is all about.

00:00:58.357 --> 00:00:59.425 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Nice to meet you.
- Man: Nice to see you.

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:00.793 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
- How's it going?
- Good.

00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:02.595 line:0%
So, what's actually--

00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:04.363 line:0%
what are we doing here?
This looks all very complicated.

00:01:04.396 --> 00:01:06.732 align:start size:72% position:28%
Oh, this is the world's
fastest camera.

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:08.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh, okay. Yeah. This is
the world's fastest camera.

00:01:08.534 --> 00:01:11.237 line:0%
There you go.
Just as easy as that.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.172 align:start position:0% line:0%
How big is
the actual camera part?

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:15.608
I can show you.

00:01:15.641 --> 00:01:18.344
That big box
is the camera itself.

00:01:18.377 --> 00:01:20.713 align:start position:0%
And here is the optics
that we designed

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:23.716 align:start size:91% position:9%
- to make the thing work.
- Very cool.

00:01:23.749 --> 00:01:25.718 align:start position:0%
A lot of times
in our YouTube comments,

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:27.553 align:start position:0%
we get asked to film
the speed of light.

00:01:27.586 --> 00:01:29.388 align:start position:0%
And I have to always
reply to people

00:01:29.421 --> 00:01:30.556 align:start position:0%
letting them know
that the speed of light,

00:01:30.589 --> 00:01:32.892 align:start position:0%
it's almost
incomprehensibly fast,

00:01:32.925 --> 00:01:35.528 align:start position:0%
and even our cameras,
under a million frames a second,

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:37.396 align:start position:0%
will never see
anything like that.

00:01:37.429 --> 00:01:40.299 align:start position:0%
Is this camera capable
of filming the speed of light?

00:01:40.332 --> 00:01:42.802 align:start size:69% position:31%
Yeah, that's basically
what we are gonna see.

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:45.671 align:start size:59% position:41%
For the example
I'm going to show,
the light will move

00:01:45.704 --> 00:01:47.807 align:end position:100%
about the length
of this bottle.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:52.445 align:start position:0%
In time, how long
does it take for light
to start here and end here?

00:01:52.478 --> 00:01:56.549 align:start size:56% position:44%
It takes about
2,000 picoseconds.

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:58.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
- That's pretty quick. Yeah.
- Pretty quick.

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:02.421 align:start position:0% line:0%
So for the audience,
it goes milliseconds,
microseconds...

00:02:02.454 --> 00:02:04.157 line:0%
- Is it nano--
- ...nanoseconds,

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.259 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- picoseconds, femtoseconds.
- Yes.

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:09.896 align:start position:0% line:0%
So we're on the sort of
pico/femto scale
with this stuff.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.931 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- We've never done
  that before, for sure.
- Yeah, no.

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:14.500 align:start position:0%
This is completely
on another level.

00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:16.469 align:start position:0%
Shall we set up
the first experiment?

00:02:16.502 --> 00:02:18.337 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Sure, yeah.
- Start with the bottle?

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Everyone should wear
  laser goggles.
- Goggles?

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Okay.
- Peng: We have some.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:24.710 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Do I look good?
- You've got side panels
  in your glasses.

00:02:24.743 --> 00:02:26.546 align:start size:81% position:19%
- You look like
  you're about to--
- You look bad-ass.

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.448 align:start position:0%
It looks like
you're about to go skiing
with a welding torch.

00:02:29.481 --> 00:02:31.951 align:start size:38% position:63%
It's just...

00:02:31.984 --> 00:02:34.287 align:start position:0%
So, I assume because
we're trying to film light,

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.922 align:start position:0%
it'll be useful
to turn all the lights off
in here, right?

00:02:35.955 --> 00:02:37.557 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yes.
- Otherwise, we'll just get...

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.791 align:start size:56% position:44%
All the ambient
light comes, yeah.

00:02:38.824 --> 00:02:40.459 align:start position:0%
Right. Okay.

00:02:40.492 --> 00:02:41.694 align:start position:0%
Let's get ready
for lights off.

00:02:41.727 --> 00:02:42.729 align:start size:28% position:72%
Yeah.

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:47.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Can you hold this for me?
- I'll hold this.

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:49.936 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Yeah, thank you.
- I'm excited.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.904 align:start position:0%
I get giddy by this
frame rate stuff.

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:54.807 size:81% position:59%
We want to see the light
propagation from the side,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.945 align:start size:84% position:16%
so we need to make sure
that the light is scattered
out of the plane of view

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.947 align:start size:53% position:47%
through the milk
molecules inside,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
then you can see
light scatter from the side.

00:03:03.015 --> 00:03:05.952 align:start position:0%
So, this is a bottle
full of water with
a bit of milk in?

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.587 align:start size:66% position:34%
So, I am going
to turn on the laser.

00:03:12.024 --> 00:03:13.960 align:start size:81% position:19%
So all you do is just move
mirrors and lenses around

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:15.528 align:start size:63% position:38%
and then it goes
to different areas?

00:03:15.561 --> 00:03:17.396 align:start position:0%
We use that to move the laser.
The laser's too big.

00:03:17.429 --> 00:03:21.500 align:start size:78% position:22%
See, the light hits
the bottom of the bottle.

00:03:21.533 --> 00:03:22.969 align:start size:56% position:44%
So it goes through
the bottle.

00:03:23.002 --> 00:03:25.905 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Is it a powerful laser?
- It's very powerful.

00:03:25.938 --> 00:03:28.674 align:start size:66% position:34%
It can basically burn
any, like, papers.

00:03:28.707 --> 00:03:30.643 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I will stay away from that.
- Yeah.

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:32.812
- Whoa, that's cool.
- You can see its glow.

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:34.847 align:start size:63% position:38%
The first thing
I'm going to capture

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.850 align:start size:59% position:41%
is the static image

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:38.884 align:start position:0%
as our reference.

00:03:38.917 --> 00:03:41.287 align:start position:0% line:0%
For today,
I'm going to try...

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:42.421 line:0%
- Excellent.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:43.689 align:start position:0%
I mean, I remember
when I was excited

00:03:43.722 --> 00:03:45.524 size:78% position:39%
when we started shooting,

00:03:45.557 --> 00:03:47.260 align:start position:0%
we moved from 1,000
frames a second

00:03:47.293 --> 00:03:49.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
- to 28,000 frames a second.
- That was a big jump.

00:03:49.628 --> 00:03:53.399
Okay, we're pretty much done
with the water bottle.

00:03:53.432 --> 00:03:55.368 align:start size:50% position:50%
I'd like to take
a photo of this.

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.370 align:start size:81% position:19%
As you can see,
we can only see the light.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.372 align:start size:75% position:25%
We cannot see the bottle
and the label on it.

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:02.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah.
- So, finally, in the movie,
  we may want to overlap

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:05.711 align:start size:69% position:31%
both the bottle itself
and the light.

00:04:05.744 --> 00:04:07.880 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Like, composite
  a real picture.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:10.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you just take a photo
on your phone and that can--
you can do that?

00:04:10.582 --> 00:04:14.887 align:start size:69% position:31%
Yeah, you can just use
software to overlap
these two things.

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.856 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Gav: Neat.
- Dan: You're in
  the photograph.

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:18.358
Just photo-bombing
the bottle.

00:04:20.626 --> 00:04:23.029 align:start position:0%
Okay, let's watch back
our bottle.

00:04:23.062 --> 00:04:26.032 align:start position:0%
This took eight hours
to process,

00:04:26.065 --> 00:04:28.901 size:75% position:38%
during which time I grew
a slightly longer beard.

00:04:28.934 --> 00:04:30.436 align:start size:75% position:25%
I had a haircut as well.

00:04:31.937 --> 00:04:34.740 align:start position:0%
All right, here we go.

00:04:34.773 --> 00:04:36.575
 Dan:<i> Okay, so what
 we're seeing here</i>

00:04:36.608 --> 00:04:39.779
<i> is the bottle's just been
 comped in, basically.</i>

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:42.615
 Gav:<i> Yeah, this camera only
 detects the light itself,</i>

00:04:42.648 --> 00:04:45.918 align:start position:0% line:0%
which is like
a blue-ish laser light,

00:04:45.951 --> 00:04:48.421 align:start position:0% line:0%
which is why you don't
really see anything else

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:49.755 align:start position:0% line:0%
other than the light
looking like that.

00:04:49.788 --> 00:04:53.659 align:start position:0% line:0%
And then we comp in
the picture of the bottle.

00:04:53.692 --> 00:04:55.061
<i> In the room
 with my actual eye,</i>

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:56.929
<i> it looked like
 it was constantly lit up,</i>

00:04:56.962 --> 00:04:58.531
<i> but here we're able to follow</i>

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:01.000
<i> the light moving
 through the bottle.</i>

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:03.869
<i> It may not look like it,
 but this is actually real.</i>

00:05:03.902 --> 00:05:05.971
Dan:<i> It's refracting the photons
 and that's why you can see it.</i>

00:05:06.004 --> 00:05:07.740
<i> But when it's just
 going through the air,</i>

00:05:07.773 --> 00:05:09.575
<i> there's nothing to actually
 reflect the light.</i>

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:11.877
Gav:<i> Yeah, it's only
 showing up in the bottle.</i>

00:05:11.910 --> 00:05:15.815
<i> It is interesting.
 It almost looks like sort of
 an '80s film effect.</i>

00:05:15.848 --> 00:05:17.550 align:start size:63% position:38%
It does, doesn't it?
It looks like--

00:05:17.583 --> 00:05:19.118 align:start position:0%
Like some sort of ghost
flying into the room.

00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:20.786 size:88% position:56%
But actually, that is light.

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:23.622
Gav:<i> Isn't that weird?
 Look at the scale.</i>

00:05:23.655 --> 00:05:26.892
<i> Every frame seems to be
 ten picoseconds.</i>

00:05:26.925 --> 00:05:28.627
<i> And we're just
 sort of casually</i>

00:05:28.660 --> 00:05:29.962
<i> watching this light
 go left to right</i>

00:05:29.995 --> 00:05:31.797
<i> through the bottle,
 but in reality,</i>

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.799
<i> the light is moving
 a million times faster</i>

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.801
<i> than a bullet.</i>

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:38.471 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Dan:<i> What a mental subject. </i>
-<i> Yeah.</i>

00:05:38.504 --> 00:05:40.639
Gav:<i> Okay, so we've shot light
 through milk.</i>

00:05:40.672 --> 00:05:42.141 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Next experiment?
- Yep.

00:05:42.174 --> 00:05:45.811
For this experiment,
we designed a special cavity.

00:05:45.844 --> 00:05:47.480
We call it a chaotic cavity.

00:05:47.513 --> 00:05:50.049 align:start size:63% position:38%
When light comes in
the cavity,

00:05:50.082 --> 00:05:51.984 align:start size:75% position:25%
it will bounce back
and forth multiple times

00:05:52.017 --> 00:05:54.553 align:start size:81% position:19%
by the mirrors surrounding
in the cavity.

00:05:54.586 --> 00:05:57.056 align:start size:97% position:3%
- You're almost
  trapping light inside the--
- Yeah. Yeah, exactly.

00:05:57.089 --> 00:05:59.058 align:start position:0%
What's the purpose of this?
This egg thing?

00:05:59.091 --> 00:06:02.061
This is to create
a water vapor

00:06:02.094 --> 00:06:04.830 size:84% position:58%
surrounding the environment
so that light scatters out.

00:06:04.863 --> 00:06:07.600 align:start size:88% position:13%
- The same thing, like--
- Oh, so you can sort of
  see a little bit.

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:11.570 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Makes sense.
- Yeah, so this is how
  the system works.

00:06:11.603 --> 00:06:14.374 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Turn it on.
- All right, let's go do
  the "experimon."

00:06:16.208 --> 00:06:18.544 align:start size:75% position:25%
How long did it take you
to learn to use this?

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:21.080
Uh, maybe a few months
to get used--

00:06:21.113 --> 00:06:23.082 align:start size:81% position:19%
- A few months
  to get used to it.
- Yeah.

00:06:23.115 --> 00:06:25.851
Because it's a really
complicated system.

00:06:25.884 --> 00:06:28.687 align:start size:75% position:25%
It's really complicated,
is it? I hadn't noticed.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.391
( both muttering )

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:34.860 align:start position:0%
Okay, so this is
the chaotic cavity

00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:37.496 align:start position:0%
at 100 billion frames
a second.

00:06:37.529 --> 00:06:39.064 align:start size:56% position:44%
Just like nothing.

00:06:39.097 --> 00:06:42.635 line:0%
Gav:<i> Once again, the duration
 of this video is--</i>

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:43.936 line:0%
<i> you can't even
 get your head around</i>

00:06:43.969 --> 00:06:46.038 line:0%
<i> how short an amount
 of time it is.</i>

00:06:46.071 --> 00:06:48.073 line:0%
Dan:<i> That is amazing.</i>

00:06:48.106 --> 00:06:49.708 line:0%
Gav:
<i> When we were in the room,</i>

00:06:49.741 --> 00:06:51.510 line:0%
<i> it looked like
 the whole thing was glowing,</i>

00:06:51.543 --> 00:06:53.179 line:0%
<i> but now we can see
 the individual pulse of light</i>

00:06:53.212 --> 00:06:55.548 line:0%
<i> bouncing around this thing.</i>

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:57.750 line:0%
<i> Looks like a weird version
 of "Pong."</i>

00:06:59.084 --> 00:07:01.053 align:start size:88% position:13%
This is one femtosecond
of laser pulse,

00:07:01.086 --> 00:07:02.555 align:start size:88% position:13%
so it's as if
you just went like...

00:07:02.588 --> 00:07:05.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
- with a laser.
- A femtosecond pulse.

00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:09.128 line:0%
Gav:<i> And if you pause it,
 you can see it's just
 a dot of light.</i>

00:07:09.161 --> 00:07:11.564 line:0%
 Dan:<i> And it's comped in
 the shape of the mirrors.</i>

00:07:11.597 --> 00:07:13.065 align:start size:66% position:34%
I wonder if you could
actually build,

00:07:13.098 --> 00:07:15.067 align:end position:100%
like, a big maze
to get it around.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.069 align:start size:72% position:28%
Do, like, a little maze
and try to get it in.

00:07:17.102 --> 00:07:18.704 align:start size:78% position:22%
- World's fastest
  maze completion.
- Yeah.

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:20.239 align:start size:69% position:31%
- ( trills )
- Light.

00:07:20.272 --> 00:07:23.075 line:0%
Dan:<i> Oh, it almost went in
 the corner there.</i>

00:07:23.108 --> 00:07:25.211 line:0%
Gav:<i> It is. It's like the DVD
 screensaver, isn't it? </i>

00:07:25.244 --> 00:07:26.745 line:0%
<i> You just want it to go
 straight in the corner.</i>

00:07:26.778 --> 00:07:28.781 line:0%
Dan:<i> Nearly.</i>

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:30.749 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- So this one's 100 billion?
- Yeah.

00:07:30.782 --> 00:07:33.953 align:start size:72% position:28%
Should we see what
500 billion looks like?

00:07:33.986 --> 00:07:35.921 align:start size:88% position:13%
- All right, so the area
  is quite small.
- Yes.

00:07:35.954 --> 00:07:38.491 align:start position:0%
So there's no way
that we can stand in and be
filmed by this camera.

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:42.595 size:88% position:44% line:0%
But alternative solution--
little mini-figurines of us.

00:07:42.628 --> 00:07:45.231 align:start size:69% position:31% line:0%
Wait, why is mine--
oh, for goodness sake.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:47.933 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Again, every time.
- So we'll put them on there.

00:07:47.966 --> 00:07:50.036 align:start size:75% position:25% line:0%
- Flipping heck.
- All right.

00:07:52.137 --> 00:07:55.674 align:start size:78% position:22%
So, in this experiment,
instead of shooting light
from the side,

00:07:55.707 --> 00:07:58.611 size:84% position:58%
I've changed the beam path
to bounce back this mirror,

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:00.779 align:start size:69% position:31%
this mirror,
and use a concave lens

00:08:00.812 --> 00:08:03.249 align:start size:66% position:34%
to expand the beam
to shoot at an angle.

00:08:03.282 --> 00:08:05.084 align:start position:0%
So this is more about
scattering light

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:06.986 align:start position:0%
on the surface
of the figurines?

00:08:07.019 --> 00:08:10.256
Yes, you're sweeping across
the surface of the figures.

00:08:10.289 --> 00:08:12.024 align:start position:0%
Because we're obviously
not see-through, so...

00:08:12.057 --> 00:08:13.059 align:start size:16% position:84%
Yeah.

